Threads and processes, from libc-test. A program ends, with status 0,
when its last thread calls pthread_exit, as POSIX says
(regression/pthread_exit-dtor); mlibc's left the process without
threads. A recursive mutex locked again by its owner, timed or not,
counts up; an error-checking one fails with EDEADLK; unlocking one
held by another thread fails with EPERM and changes nothing
(functional/pthread_mutex). A mutex taken without waiting no longer
sets its waiters flag, so unlocking it makes no futex system call, as
with glibc and musl; mlibc's woke on every unlock, which cost a system
call each time and woke threads still waiting on a mutex since
initialized again at the same address. pthread_create fails with
EAGAIN when the new thread's TLS cannot be allocated
(regression/pthread_create-oom). pthread_atfork's handlers are the
process's, so a fork from any thread runs them (mlibc kept them in the
registering thread's TCB), and a fork that fails runs the parent's
handlers and keeps its errno (regression/pthread_atfork-errno-clobber).
daemon opens /dev/null and changes directory before it forks, as musl
does, so its caller sees those errors (regression/daemon-failure).
Meant for upstream.

